{"name":"Coral Dune","hex":"#fcd5c5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"coral","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of many species of marine invertebrates in the class Anthozoa, most of which build hard calcium carbonate skeletons and form colonies, or a colony belonging to one of those species.","A hard substance made of the skeletons of these organisms."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of coral.","Having the orange-pink color of coral."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/coral"},{"term":"dune","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A ridge or hill of sand piled up by currents of wind or water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dune"}],"entries":[{"title":"Coral","blocks":[{"text":"Corals are colonial marine invertebrates within the subphylum Anthozoa of the phylum Cnidaria. They typically form compact colonies of many identical individual polyps. Coral species include the important reef builders that inhabit tropical oceans and secrete calcium carbonate to form a hard skeleton."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Coral_Outcrop_Flynn_Reef.jpg/330px-Coral_Outcrop_Flynn_Reef.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coral"},{"title":"Dune","blocks":[{"text":"A dune is a landform composed of wind- or water-driven sand. It typically takes the form of a mound, ridge, or hill. An area with dunes is called a dune system or a dune complex. A large dune complex is called a dune field, while broad, flat regions covered with wind-swept sand or dunes, with little or no vegetation, are called ergs or sand seas. Dunes occur in different shapes and sizes, but most kinds of dunes are longer on the stoss (upflow) side, where the sand is pushed up the dune, and have a shorter slip face in the lee side. The valley or trough between dunes is called a dune slack."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Dunas_de_Maspalomas.jpg/330px-Dunas_de_Maspalomas.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dune"},{"title":"Coral (color)","blocks":[{"text":"The various tones of the color coral are orange, red and pink representations of the colors of those cnidarians known as precious corals."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coral_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Coral_polyp.jpg/330px-Coral_polyp.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Coral_polyp.jpg/330px-Coral_polyp.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Anatomy of a stony coral polyp","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACoral_polyp.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Montastrea_cavernosa.jpg/500px-Montastrea_cavernosa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Montastrea_cavernosa.jpg/1280px-Montastrea_cavernosa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Montastrea_cavernosa.jpg/500px-Montastrea_cavernosa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Montastraea cavernosa polyps with tentacles extended","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMontastrea_cavernosa.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/The_general_organization_of_the_scleractinian_coral_soft_tissue_and_underlying_skeleton.png/500px-The_general_organization_of_the_scleractinian_coral_soft_tissue_and_underlying_skeleton.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77